--- a/docs/Nanz_Language_Book.md
+++ b/docs/Nanz_Language_Book.md
@@ -262,20 +262,28 @@ a >  b   a >= b
 
 ```nanz
 !a       // logical NOT (unary)
+a && b   // logical AND — b is evaluated only if a is true
+a || b   // logical OR  — b is evaluated only if a is false
 ```
 
+`&&` and `||` short-circuit and produce a `bool` (0 or 1). Any non-zero operand
+counts as true. They bind more loosely than comparisons and bitwise operators,
+so `lo <= x && x < hi || x == 0` reads as `((lo <= x) && (x < hi)) || (x == 0)`.
+
 **Precedence table** (highest to lowest):
 
 | Level | Operators |
 |-------|-----------|
-| 8 | `*` `/` `%` |
-| 7 | `+` `-` |
-| 6 | `<<` `>>` |
-| 5 | `<` `<=` `>` `>=` |
-| 4 | `==` `!=` |
-| 3 | `&` |
-| 2 | `^` (XOR) |
-| 1 | `\|` |
+| 10 | `*` `/` `%` |
+| 9 | `+` `-` |
+| 8 | `<<` `>>` |
+| 7 | `<` `<=` `>` `>=` |
+| 6 | `==` `!=` |
+| 5 | `&` |
+| 4 | `^` (XOR) |
+| 3 | `\|` |
+| 2 | `&&` |
+| 1 | `\|\|` |
 
 Parentheses override precedence: `(a + b) * c`.
 
